---
/**
 * Case study: Redesigning Global Time Management (Darwinbox).
 * Structure: 5-stage executive arc (Context & Complexity → Systems Thinking
 * → Solution & Visual Walkthrough → Validated in Market → Impact &
 * Operational Outcomes → Judgment).
 *
 * Constraints: no demo/engagement metrics, client names anonymised to
 * industry + country/region, no Gartner Magic Quadrant framing (that is a
 * different Gartner product; we have no MQ placement data, only Critical
 * Capabilities / Peer Insights scores).
 *
 * Gartner figures, updated 2026-11-16 from a sourced table (2022–2025 from
 * internal historical tracking, 2026 from Gartner, August 2026): the
 * Workforce Management capability score went 2.9 (2022) → 2.9 (2023) → 3.2
 * (2024) → 3.5 (2025) → 3.7 (2026). Separately, Darwinbox ranked #2 of 11
 * vendors globally in Applied AI in the 2026 Gartner Critical Capabilities
 * for HCM Suites report, ahead of Workday, SAP and UKG; that ranking is
 * company-wide, not attributable to this feature alone, and is presented as
 * such (hero card + hero card only, not folded into the Workforce
 * Management timeline below).
 *
 * The hero film originally had a closing stats card baked into its last
 * ~15 seconds with fabricated engagement numbers ("1.16M dashboard plays",
 * "73.1% manager play rate"), numbers that never appear anywhere else in
 * this case study and read as demo-metric filler. Removed on 2026-11-16 by
 * cutting that whole tail segment (ffmpeg, frame-accurate re-encode, audio
 * preserved with a matching fade), not by patching pixels inside a frame.
 * The film now ends where the real product walkthrough ends.
 */
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CaseHero from "../../components/CaseHero.astro";
import FilmPlayer from "../../components/FilmPlayer.astro";

const IMG = "/images/work/time-management";
---

<Base title="Redesigning Global Time Management | Kishore Sudhamalla">
  <CaseHero
    eyebrow="Case study 01 · Darwinbox"
    title="Redesigning Global Time Management."
    lede="Time, attendance and payroll-adjacent infrastructure for a platform operating in 120+ countries: daily, compliance-dependent data with zero dedicated redesign investment in eight years."
    meta={[
      { k: "Role", v: "Senior Manager, UX" },
      { k: "Team", v: "2 designers · 3 PMs · 2 frontend devs" },
      { k: "Timeline", v: "2023 – 2024" },
      { k: "Platform", v: "Darwinbox HCM · 120+ countries" },
    ]}
    video={`${IMG}/tm-manager-dashboard`}
    poster={`${IMG}/tm-manager-dashboard-poster.jpg`}
    alt="Time Management manager dashboard"
    cards={[
      { eyebrow: "Gartner · Workforce Management", value: "3.7", label: "2.9 in 2022 to 3.7 in 2026", note: "Flat at 2.9 for two years, then up every year this redesign has been live. Gartner Critical Capabilities for HCM Suites." },
      { eyebrow: "Gartner · Critical Capabilities, 2026", value: "#2", label: "Globally in Applied AI, of 11 vendors", note: "Ahead of Workday, SAP and UKG. A company-wide ranking, not specific to this feature." },
    ]}
  />

  <section class="container-pg r-film" data-reveal>
    <p class="r-case-label">Film · with sound</p>
    <div class="r-film-frame">
      <FilmPlayer src={`${IMG}/tm-hero-film.mp4`} poster={`${IMG}/tm-hero-film-poster.jpg`} label="A short animated film of the Time Management redesign: the module before, one front door, and the employee, manager and HRBP dashboards it opened into" />
    </div>
    <p class="r-film-note">About 55 seconds, with sound. The dashboards are rebuilt from the shipped screens; names are demo data.</p>
  </section>

  <article class="container-pg r-case">
    <!-- Stage 2: Context & enterprise complexity -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">Context &amp; complexity</p>
      <h2>An operational system, not a feature backlog.</h2>
      <p>
        Time Management is Darwinbox's time, attendance, leave and payroll-dependent module. Every customer configures its own mix of compliance rules, shift structures and approval chains, so the same screens have to hold up under materially different conditions from one tenant to the next.
      </p>

      <div class="r-quick-stats">
        <div><p class="r-quick-stats-v">120+</p><p class="r-quick-stats-l">Countries</p></div>
        <div><p class="r-quick-stats-v">8 yrs</p><p class="r-quick-stats-l">Since last redesign</p></div>
        <div><p class="r-quick-stats-v">1M+</p><p class="r-quick-stats-l">Daily users</p></div>
      </div>

      <p>
        The difficulty was never any single screen. Attendance, leave and payroll depend on each other, and every exception, a missed clock-in, an unapproved leave, a roster conflict, sits upstream of a payroll run. Eight years of incremental requests had produced a system that was powerful but dense enough that managers absorbed the cost as exception fatigue, constantly re-deriving what needed attention instead of being told.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Stage 3: Systems thinking & information architecture -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">Systems thinking</p>
      <h2>Untangling the data hierarchy before a screen got drawn.</h2>
      <p>
        Primary research with three Indian enterprise clients (manufacturing, e-grocery and food retail), plus secondary analysis across Kronos, Workday, BambooHR and ZingHR, surfaced the same structural gap: no central view of time data existed for any role. Everything lived behind transactional flows, so a manager or employee had to already know what they were looking for before they could find it. One enterprise manager put it directly:
      </p>
      <blockquote>
        "If I had a magic wand to change one thing about the Time module,
        I'd add dashboards for employees and managers."
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        The real problem was not a missing feature. There was no information layer above the policies, shift rosters and exception queues, no single view that resolved "what do I need to decide right now" for any of the three roles depending on it. Before drawing a screen, we mapped how those data points related, so the dashboard could read the system instead of sitting bolted on top of it.
      </p>
      <p class="r-sys-note">
        This was also the platform's first ground-up revamp, a pivot from building to listening. So the layout study wasn't scoped to Time Management alone: it was run to settle a pattern every module could reuse.
      </p>

      <div class="r-decisions">
        <div class="r-decision-prose">
          <p class="r-decision-tag">Decision 1</p>
          <p>
            A dashboard needed primary time data plus the context that makes it actionable: schedule, team comparison, exceptions, without splitting a manager's attention. A 3-column layout, one column per concern, looked more flexible on paper but meant scanning three places to answer one question. We shipped 2 columns instead, one primary view and one context panel. Less sits on screen at once, but there's a clear order of what to look at first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="r-decision-visual">
          <figure>
            <div class="r-decision-visual-frame">
              <img src={`${IMG}/image-1783857502682.png`} alt="3-column dashboard layout, exploration pulled from global layout research" loading="lazy" />
            </div>
            <figcaption>Considered: 3 columns</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure>
            <div class="r-decision-visual-frame">
              <img src={`${IMG}/image-1783857498993.png`} alt="2-column dashboard layout, exploration pulled from global layout research" loading="lazy" />
            </div>
            <figcaption>Shipped: 2 columns</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>

        <div class="r-decision-prose">
          <p class="r-decision-tag">Decision 2</p>
          <p>
            Employees, managers and HRBPs each needed a different view of the same time data. The existing module handled that with separate, inconsistent screens per role. We built one dashboard framework instead, configured per role and permission set. It cost more upfront than shipping one role's view first, but left one information model to maintain instead of three. All three dashboards shipped from it, with zero module switching.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="r-decision-prose r-pattern-reuse">
          <p class="r-decision-tag">Beyond this module</p>
          <p class="r-pattern-lead">One layout, reused across the platform.</p>
          <p>
            Neither decision stayed local to Time Management. The 2-column structure, one workspace plus one context panel, was formalized into a reusable pattern now standard for manager views elsewhere, including Journeys (status and assignment) and Learning Management (what's assigned to each report). Same bones, different data.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="r-decision-visual r-decision-visual--wide">
          <figure>
            <div class="r-decision-visual-frame" data-parallax>
              <img src={`${IMG}/pattern-reuse/time-management-pattern.webp`} alt="Time Management manager view: reportee roster on the left, a summary of present days, leaves and overtime plus insights on the right" loading="lazy" />
            </div>
            <figcaption>Time Management — Reportee Analysis</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure>
            <div class="r-decision-visual-frame" data-parallax>
              <img src={`${IMG}/pattern-reuse/journeys-pattern.webp`} alt="Journeys manager view: reportee roster on the left, ongoing journeys and assignable journeys on the right" loading="lazy" />
            </div>
            <figcaption>Journeys — Reportee Journey Status</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Stage 4: The solution & visual walkthrough -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">Solution &amp; visual walkthrough</p>
      <h2>What shipped, and what it took off someone's plate.</h2>

      <div class="r-craft-compare">
        <figure>
          <div class="r-media-frame">
            <img src={`${IMG}/before/old-attendance.webp`} alt="Attendance module before the redesign" loading="lazy" />
          </div>
          <figcaption>Before: attendance with no information layer above it, transactional screens only, no synthesis for the person reading them.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <div class="r-media-frame">
            <img src={`${IMG}/before/old-roster.webp`} alt="Roster view before the redesign" loading="lazy" />
          </div>
          <figcaption>Before: the roster view carried the same density problem, with no exception surfaced above the raw data.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <div class="r-craft-grid">
        <figure>
          <div class="r-media-frame">
            <video autoplay loop muted playsinline preload="metadata" poster={`${IMG}/tm-employee-poster.jpg`}>
              <source src={`${IMG}/tm-employee.webm`} type="video/webm" />
              <source src={`${IMG}/tm-employee.mp4`} type="video/mp4" />
            </video>
          </div>
          <figcaption>Employee dashboard, shipped. One view in place of per-task screens, cutting the clicks needed to answer "where do things stand."</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <div class="r-media-frame">
            <video autoplay loop muted playsinline preload="metadata" poster={`${IMG}/tm-manager-poster.jpg`}>
              <source src={`${IMG}/tm-manager.webm`} type="video/webm" />
              <source src={`${IMG}/tm-manager.mp4`} type="video/mp4" />
            </video>
          </div>
          <figcaption>Manager dashboard, shipped. Exceptions and team context surface automatically, closing the exception-handling loop on-screen instead of in a manager's head.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <div class="r-media-frame">
            <video autoplay loop muted playsinline preload="metadata" poster={`${IMG}/tm-hrbp-poster.jpg`}>
              <source src={`${IMG}/tm-hrbp.webm`} type="video/webm" />
              <source src={`${IMG}/tm-hrbp.mp4`} type="video/mp4" />
            </video>
          </div>
          <figcaption>HRBP dashboard, shipped. Org-level attendance and leave patterns on screen, replacing a manually assembled report.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <p class="r-craft-note">
        Mobile and responsive behaviour: the product is used globally across desktop and mobile by frontline and field employees.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Stage 5a: validated in market, feeding into impact -->
    <section class="r-case-section r-wide" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">Validated in market</p>
      <h2>Taken to enterprise clients across SEA and MENA before rollout.</h2>
      <p>
        Delivered over roughly a year with a staff designer and myself hands-on, owning design direction and client validation, alongside two frontend developers and three product managers covering Leave, Attendance, and Scheduling &amp; Timesheets. Before global rollout we ran a UX roadshow across Singapore, the Philippines, Indonesia and Dubai, UAE, presenting the shipped direction to client teams and collecting their reactions in the room.
      </p>

      <p class="r-sub-label">Who we presented to</p>
      <div class="r-roster">
        {[
          { industry: "Education", countries: [{ country: "Singapore" }] },
          { industry: "Banking & Financial Services", countries: [{ country: "Singapore" }, { country: "Philippines", count: 2 }] },
          { industry: "Manufacturing", countries: [{ country: "Philippines" }] },
          { industry: "Multi-industry", countries: [{ country: "Philippines" }, { country: "Dubai, UAE" }] },
          { industry: "Airline & Transportation", countries: [{ country: "Philippines" }] },
          { industry: "Healthcare", countries: [{ country: "Dubai, UAE" }] },
          { industry: "Maritime & Logistics Consultancy", countries: [{ country: "Dubai, UAE" }] },
        ].map((c) => (
          <div class="r-roster-item">
            <p class="r-roster-industry">{c.industry}</p>
            <p class="r-roster-country">
              {c.countries.map((e) => `${e.country}${e.count ? ` (${e.count})` : ""}`).join(" · ")}
            </p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <p class="r-sub-label">On the ground</p>
      <div class="r-photos-collage">
        {[
          { f: "session-studio-tiered", alt: "Presenting the redesigned time dashboard to a client team in a tiered studio space", hero: true },
          { f: "session-boardroom-dashboard", alt: "Walking a client team through the manager dashboard in a boardroom" },
          { f: "session-classroom", alt: "A larger roadshow session presented to client staff in a classroom setting" },
          { f: "session-manila-boardroom", alt: "A working session reviewing the designs with a client team" },
          { f: "busy-bees-team", alt: "The team at Busy Bees International after a roadshow session" },
        ].map((p) => (
          <figure class={`r-photo${p.hero ? " r-photo--hero" : ""}`}>
            <img src={`${IMG}/roadshow/${p.f}.webp`} alt={p.alt} loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          </figure>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="r-photos-strip">
        {[
          { f: "toyota-team", alt: "The team with client stakeholders at Toyota" },
          { f: "client-team-group", alt: "Group photo with a client team following a session", pos: "bottom" },
          { f: "ghassan-aboud-group-session", ext: "jpg", alt: "Presenting to the Ghassan Aboud Group team in their Dubai boardroom" },
          { f: "dubai-expo-city-team", ext: "jpg", alt: "The team on site at Expo City Dubai during the MENA roadshow" },
        ].map((p) => (
          <figure class="r-photo">
            <img src={`${IMG}/roadshow/${p.f}.${p.ext ?? "webp"}`} alt={p.alt} loading="lazy" decoding="async" style={p.pos ? `object-position: center ${p.pos};` : undefined} />
          </figure>
        ))}
      </div>

      <p class="r-sub-label">What they validated</p>
      <div class="r-validated">
        {[
          { t: "Consolidated view", q: "“Finally, everything in one place”" },
          { t: "Manager overview", q: "Team context at a glance" },
          { t: "Clock-in widget", q: "Placement felt natural" },
          { t: "Leave lineup", q: "Who's out, who's in" },
        ].map((v) => (
          <div class="r-validated-item">
            <p class="r-validated-t">{v.t}</p>
            <p class="r-validated-q">{v.q}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <p class="r-sub-label">Post-release review with clients</p>
      <div class="r-review">
        <div class="r-review-col">
          <p class="r-review-head">What went well</p>
          <ul>
            <li>All functionality aligned with user intent</li>
            <li>Consolidated view of leave and attendance</li>
            <li>Manager overview of all their reports</li>
            <li>Evident clock-in widget to address missed ones</li>
            <li>Leave lineup identified as a standout feature</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="r-review-quote">“Now everything's in one place, and we look forward to enabling this.” <span>Manufacturing client, Philippines</span></p>
          <p class="r-review-quote">“Time module is more detailed than before.” <span>Roadshow client feedback</span></p>
        </div>
        <div class="r-review-col">
          <p class="r-review-head">Areas of improvement / asks</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Dashboard utility with just leave data when attendance is not enabled</li>
            <li>Offline capability in mobile: leave application for desk-less employees with limited connectivity</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="r-review-quote">“Time dashboard with only leave data, what would it look like?” <span>Education client, Singapore</span></p>
          <p class="r-review-note">Most clients did not have the employee-shifts use case. Those that did asked for a separate deep-dive call on scheduler and timesheets.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Stage 5b: Impact & operational outcomes -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">Impact &amp; operational outcomes</p>
      <h2>What it replaced for a manager, and what moved as a result.</h2>

      <p>
        Before this, a manager's view of their team's time wasn't a dashboard. It was a report they had to go build: pull attendance data, cross-reference who was on leave, scan rows by hand to catch anything that needed attention. That took real time, every time, and it was easy to miss a pattern buried in a spreadsheet.
      </p>
      <p>
        The redesigned dashboard puts that work on the screen instead of on the manager. Pending approvals, who hasn't logged in yet, who's been working without a break, who to flag for a pattern: all of it surfaces on its own, system-detected rather than manager-derived. The shift is from "go find out" to "it's already here":
      </p>

      <figure class="r-impact" data-impact>
        <div class="r-impact-img" data-parallax>
          <img src={`${IMG}/tm-manager-overview-full.webp`} alt="Manager Time Management overview: pending requests, today's logged-in status, team attendance chart, upcoming time off, team insights, my team vs org, and reportee analysis" loading="lazy" />
          <button type="button" class="r-impact-dot" data-idx="1" style="--x: 45%; --y: 13%;" aria-label="Highlight: approvals, with context"><span>1</span></button>
          <button type="button" class="r-impact-dot" data-idx="2" style="--x: 92%; --y: 12%;" aria-label="Highlight: who still needs a nudge"><span>2</span></button>
          <button type="button" class="r-impact-dot" data-idx="3" style="--x: 45%; --y: 32%;" aria-label="Highlight: exceptions visible at a glance"><span>3</span></button>
          <button type="button" class="r-impact-dot" data-idx="4" style="--x: 86%; --y: 40%;" aria-label="Highlight: patterns the manager didn't have to look for"><span>4</span></button>
        </div>
        <ol class="r-impact-legend">
          <li data-idx="1"><b>Approvals, with context.</b> Each leave request shows when it was applied for, so a manager isn't approving blind. No separate lookup to see how recent or how urgent it is.</li>
          <li data-idx="2"><b>Who still needs a nudge.</b> "Not logged in" is a filtered list, not something a manager has to cross-check against a roster by hand.</li>
          <li data-idx="3"><b>Exceptions visible at a glance.</b> Absences and anomalies sit inside the same attendance view as everyone present. No separate exception report to pull.</li>
          <li data-idx="4"><b>Patterns the manager didn't have to look for.</b> The system flags things like employees overdue for time off or repeatedly clocking in late, judgment calls that used to depend on a manager noticing, now surfaced automatically.</li>
        </ol>
      </figure>

      <div class="r-outcome-row">
        <div class="r-stat-card">
          <div class="r-stat-head">
            <span class="r-stat-icon" aria-hidden="true">G</span>
            <div>
              <p class="r-stat-brand">Gartner</p>
              <p class="r-stat-l">Workforce Management score, out of 5</p>
            </div>
            <span class="r-stat-delta">+28% <small>since 2022</small></span>
          </div>
          <div class="r-stat-bars">
            {[
              { y: "2022", v: "2.9", pct: 58, after: false },
              { y: "2023", v: "2.9", pct: 58, after: false },
              { y: "2024", v: "3.2", pct: 64, after: true },
              { y: "2025", v: "3.5", pct: 70, after: true },
              { y: "2026", v: "3.7", pct: 74, after: true, current: true },
            ].map((r) => (
              <div class={`r-stat-bar-item${r.current ? " r-stat-bar-item--current" : ""}`}>
                <span class="r-stat-bar-label">{r.y}</span>
                <div class="r-stat-bar-track"><div class={`r-stat-bar-fill${r.after ? " r-stat-bar-fill--after" : ""}`} style={`--pct: ${r.pct}%;`}></div></div>
                <span class="r-stat-bar-val">{r.v}</span>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
          <p class="r-stat-note">Flat 2022–2023, then up every year since this redesign shipped. 2026 figure: Gartner Critical Capabilities for HCM Suites, Aug 2026.</p>
        </div>

        <blockquote class="r-quote-card">
          <span class="r-quote-mark" aria-hidden="true">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
              <path d="M7.5 6C5 6 3 8 3 10.8c0 2.3 1.6 4 3.8 4 .4 0 .8-.05 1.1-.15C7.3 17.3 5.9 18.7 4 19.4l.9 1.8c3.6-1.3 6-4.3 6-8.9C10.9 8.6 9.4 6 7.5 6zm9.6 0c-2.5 0-4.5 2-4.5 4.8 0 2.3 1.6 4 3.8 4 .4 0 .8-.05 1.1-.15-.6 2.65-2 4.05-3.9 4.75l.9 1.8c3.6-1.3 6-4.3 6-8.9C20.5 8.6 19 6 17.1 6z" fill="currentColor" />
            </svg>
          </span>
          Really great job on time management, excellent UI and features. The
          main highlight for me is how it simplified direct-reportee
          attendance insight, analysis, comparison with the org, and the
          schedule planner.
          <cite>Darwinbox customer community member · 2025</cite>
        </blockquote>
      </div>

      <p class="r-sys-close">
        Scaled across the same 120+ countries and customer-specific compliance configurations described at the start: one information architecture, three roles, zero module switching, and a system built to take on the next jurisdiction without a redesign.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Closing judgment note -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">Judgment</p>
      <h2>What I'd lock in earlier next time.</h2>
      <p class="r-judgment-lead">What I would change if I ran it back.</p>
      <ul class="r-judgment-list">
        <li>
          <b>Commit to the data model earlier.</b> We lost weeks chasing the theoretical flexibility of a three-column layout. The data hierarchy had already given us the answer; we just spent too long trying to make paper flexibility win over structural clarity.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Get real-world eyes on wireframes, not polished code.</b> Taking our roadshow prototype out across Singapore, the Philippines, and Dubai delivered our highest-fidelity validation, but it landed too late in the cycle. Because the screens were nearly final, the incredible client feedback had to go into a backlog ticket instead of shaping the architecture. Showing rougher concepts sooner would have let the market actually steer the build rather than just applaud the finish line.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<script>
  // Wire the numbered dots on the manager-dashboard screenshot to their
  // matching legend lines, in both directions, by shared data-idx.
  function initImpactLinks() {
    const figure = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-impact]");
    if (!figure || figure.dataset.wired) return;
    figure.dataset.wired = "1";

    const dots = Array.from(figure.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(".r-impact-dot"));
    const items = Array.from(figure.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(".r-impact-legend li"));
    const pairsFor = (idx: string | null) => ({
      dot: dots.find((d) => d.dataset.idx === idx),
      item: items.find((i) => i.dataset.idx === idx),
    });

    const setActive = (idx: string | null) => {
      dots.forEach((d) => d.classList.toggle("is-active", d.dataset.idx === idx));
      items.forEach((i) => i.classList.toggle("is-active", i.dataset.idx === idx));
    };

    dots.forEach((d) => {
      d.addEventListener("pointerenter", () => setActive(d.dataset.idx ?? null));
      d.addEventListener("focus", () => setActive(d.dataset.idx ?? null));
      d.addEventListener("click", () => {
        const { item } = pairsFor(d.dataset.idx ?? null);
        item?.scrollIntoView({ behavior: "smooth", block: "nearest" });
      });
    });
    items.forEach((i) => {
      i.addEventListener("pointerenter", () => setActive(i.dataset.idx ?? null));
      i.addEventListener("focus", () => setActive(i.dataset.idx ?? null));
    });
    figure.addEventListener("pointerleave", () => setActive(null));
  }
  initImpactLinks();
  document.addEventListener("astro:page-load", initImpactLinks);

  // Scroll parallax across the page's images: the impact dashboard, the
  // pattern-reuse pair, decision visuals, before/after and dashboard shots,
  // and the roadshow photos. Strength scales with the element's own size
  // so a large screenshot drifts noticeably while small grid photos only
  // shift a little, keeping a packed grid from feeling jittery.
  function initParallax() {
    const els = Array.from(
      document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(
        "[data-parallax], .r-decision-visual-frame, .r-media-frame, .r-photo"
      )
    );
    if (!els.length) return;

    const reduce = window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)");
    let raf = 0;

    const apply = () => {
      raf = 0;
      if (reduce.matches) {
        els.forEach((el) => (el.style.transform = ""));
        return;
      }
      const vh = window.innerHeight;
      els.forEach((el) => {
        const r = el.getBoundingClientRect();
        if (r.bottom < 0 || r.top > vh) return;
        const center = r.top + r.height / 2;
        const progress = Math.max(-1, Math.min(1, (center - vh / 2) / vh));
        const strength = Math.max(6, Math.min(20, r.width / 24));
        el.style.transform = `translateY(${progress * -strength}px)`;
      });
    };
    const onScroll = () => {
      if (!raf) raf = requestAnimationFrame(apply);
    };
    window.addEventListener("scroll", onScroll, { passive: true });
    apply();
  }
  initParallax();
  document.addEventListener("astro:page-load", initParallax);
